\subsection{Space Efficiency}
\label{sec:eval:space}

\EQ{feasibility}{Feasibility}{Can \rush{} run circuits on small chips that are infeasible for static approaches?}
Comparing minimum feasible chips per circuit (\figref{advantage}a), we find each design needs a $1.2\times$ (LSQCA) to $3.5\times$ (DASCOT) larger chip than \rush{}, and at \rush{}'s minimum chip $30$ of $35$ benchmarks run on no baseline.
On the tightest chips ($1.5\times$ the physical minimum), \rush{} runs $29$ of $35$ circuits where the best static design (LSQCA) runs $13$, and where any baseline is feasible at all, \rush{} reaches a successful shot a median $7.2\times$ sooner.

\EQ{spaceimprovement}{Space improvement}{How much space does \rush{} save at matched time?}
To match \rush{}'s time, every static design needs $1.3$--$5.8\times$ \rush{}'s physical qubits, even when each is taken at its fastest operating point (\tabref{summary}).

